 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/3/OA/C/7/",
 "standard_text": "Multiply within 100 fluently and know all products of two one-digit numbers from memory.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 3 multiplication facts worksheets: arrays, times tables 0–10 with a strategy for every fact family, missing factors and word problems. 100 practice questions with instant feedback and a printable answer key.",
 "key_big": "Multiplication = equal groups. Learn × 2, × 5, × 10 first, then use them to find the rest.",
 "key_sub": "Every hard fact comes from an easy one: × 6 is × 5 plus one more group, and × 9 is × 10 minus one group. Know the strategy, and you never have to guess.",
 "body_html": "<h2>What multiplication means</h2><p>Multiplication is a fast way to add <b>equal groups</b>. <b>4 × 3</b> means 4 groups of 3, which is 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 12. An <b>array</b> shows the same idea with rows and columns.</p><figure class='sn-fig'><svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 140 180' width='140' height='180' role='img' aria-label='array of 4 rows and 3 columns'><circle cx='30' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='30' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='30' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='70' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='30' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='110' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='30' cy='150' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='70' cy='150' r='13' fill='#006633'/><circle cx='110' cy='150' r='13' fill='#006633'/></svg><figcaption>4 rows with 3 dots in each row: 4 × 3 = 12. Turn it sideways and you get 3 × 4 = 12. The order of the factors does not change the product.</figcaption></figure><h2>Strategies for every fact family</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Facts</th><th>Strategy</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>× 2</td><td>Double the number</td><td>7 × 2 = 7 + 7 = 14</td></tr><tr><td>× 3</td><td>Double, then add one more group</td><td>6 × 3 = 12 + 6 = 18</td></tr><tr><td>× 4</td><td>Double, then double again</td><td>7 × 4: 14, then 28</td></tr><tr><td>× 5</td><td>Half of × 10</td><td>8 × 5 = half of 80 = 40</td></tr><tr><td>× 6</td><td>× 5, then add one more group</td><td>7 × 6 = 35 + 7 = 42</td></tr><tr><td>× 7</td><td>× 5 plus × 2</td><td>8 × 7 = 40 + 16 = 56</td></tr><tr><td>× 8</td><td>Double three times</td><td>6 × 8: 12, 24, 48</td></tr><tr><td>× 9</td><td>× 10, then take one group away</td><td>7 × 9 = 70 − 7 = 63</td></tr><tr><td>× 10</td><td>Write a zero at the end</td><td>6 × 10 = 60</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>6 × 7 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5 × 7 = 35. One more group of 7: 35 + 7 = 42.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 42</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example 2</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>9 × 8 = ?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>10 × 8 = 80. Take away one group of 8: 80 − 8 = 72.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 72</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Word problem</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>There are 5 boxes with 6 pencils in each box. How many pencils?</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>5 equal groups of 6: 5 × 6 = 30.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 30 pencils</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adding the two numbers (6 × 4 = 10)</td><td>Multiply means equal groups: 6 groups of 4 = 24</td></tr><tr><td>Mixing up neighbors (7 × 8 = 54)</td><td>Use a strategy: 7 × 8 = 40 + 16 = 56</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking 0 × 5 = 5</td><td>Zero groups of anything is 0</td></tr><tr><td>Thinking 1 × 9 = 1</td><td>One group of 9 is 9</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What multiplication facts should a 3rd grader know?",
   "a": "By the end of Grade 3, students are expected to know all products of two one-digit numbers from memory, from 0 × 0 up to 9 × 9 (and the ×10 facts)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the best order to learn the times tables?",
   "a": "Start with × 2, × 5 and × 10, then × 3, × 4 and × 6, and finish with × 7, × 8 and × 9. Each set builds on the strategies from the one before."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why does 4 × 3 equal 3 × 4?",
   "a": "An array of 4 rows of 3 is the same array as 3 columns of 4 turned sideways. This is the commutative property, and it cuts the number of facts to learn almost in half."
  },
  {
   "q": "How can I practice multiplication facts at home?",
   "a": "Do one 20-question set a day, redo only the questions you missed, and print the worksheet for a timed check once a week."
  }
 ],
